The vulnerability in `@tsed/core` stems from iterating over object keys without proper validation. Standard methods like `Object.keys()` iterate over all enumerable own properties of an object, including those named `constructor`. In standard JavaScript assignment logic, when a property named `constructor` is assigned to an object (e.g., `{}`), the interpreter resolves the key via the prototype chain and triggers the internal `[Set]` mechanism on `Object.prototype.constructor`. This allows an attacker to bypass local scope and directly modify the global `Object.prototype`.

The proof of concept (PoC) below demonstrates this by sending a payload that assigns a value to the `prototype` property within the `constructor`. After the `cleanObject` function processes the data, checking `globalThis.Object.prototype['exploited']` verifies if the global prototype was modified.

